# Keep line endings LF in the repository regardless of the contributor's platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true produces CRLF working files, and any
# that get consumed as data rather than compiled will carry the CRLF through. `minimal.tlv` is the
# concrete case: it is copied into ~/.vscode-makerchip/resources/ and compiled, and SandPiper
# wants LF. (extension.ts also normalizes CRLF out of TL-Verilog source before dispatching a
# compile, which covers user files; this covers ours.)
#
# `text=auto` means git decides what is text and normalizes it to LF on commit; `eol=lf` means it
# checks out as LF everywhere too, so a Windows contributor sees the same bytes CI does.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the file whose line endings actually reach a compiler.
*.tlv text eol=lf

# Never touch these — normalization would corrupt them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.vsix binary
